    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Think of it like this: the soil solution is a busy marketplace, and the micronutrients are the valuable goods. When the pH is optimal, the iron and manganese are displayed as easily accessible, soluble items—like fresh, bright red apples ready for sale. The vine's root cells are the specialized shoppers with specific baskets (transporters) designed to pick up those particular apples. When the pH rises too high, those apples don't stay on the display; they chemically clump together into hard, unusable bricks (insoluble precipitates) right there in the marketplace. Even if the shoppers (transporters) are perfectly ready, they can't pick up a brick.",
      "source": "target",
